and what do you make of that lenders  being too complacent well what I'd say  is we we are not complacent I heard Alec  Ferguson once they complacence is a  disease and so we should all avoid that  at all costs I think that it's really  important that the Bank of England are  making the comments that they are  interest rates are obviously low wages  are flat and as a result people are keen  to borrow more and we just all have to  make sure that people can afford to pay  back what they do borrow and a purging  money we're very very focused on making  sure we lend responsibly to our  customers and they understand what  they've got and so far you know that's  going very well it's very interesting  the Bank of England's prudential  regulatory arm recently decided against  capping effective interest rates on  cards so I mean they could have done  that could they if they were really  worried I think that if you remember a  couple of years ago there was a concern  about the by solute housing market in  the Bank of England intervened the PRA  intervened just to say that they had  these concerns and often that's I  suspect what it takes in order to get  all of the banks really focused on are  we doing the right thing do we think  we've got a problem here and so I think  that their comments that they're making  are well timed and you know definitely  in a place that means that all of the  banks I'm sure will be looking at the  quality of their lending in the way in  which they lend we always look at that  I'm sure others do too today  particularly after the speech made last  night  are you actually turning away a lot of  credit applicants then from our point of  view we accept customers that meet our  credit criteria and then we do turn away  those that don't I mean other banks do  offer customers a different deal perhaps  a more expensive deal but from my point  of view we think that it is responsible  to say to customers this is the product  and this is the hurdle that you have to  achieve in order to get it and if you  don't then it's probably not right for  you so what about this warning that  you've issued on the housing market  today I mean it's it's rattled some some  of your investors today hasn't it  well do you - it wasn't meant to be a  warning on their housing market at all  from our point of view we were just  giving a balance that looked to what's  been an excellent first half so we've  had the best ever half year for  mortgages i'm intermedia business is  strong we've got new products and so  from our perspective all is good the  housing market certainly flattens  slightly growing on last year but we're  just looking to the future and saying  you know we do say that there's some  tempering of house  and consumers we think just need to  think about that as they take out  mortgages so they don't borrow too much  you've actually said I think house price  weaknesses in certain regions is that  London you're talking about well I think  we've seen a lot of press and media  comments that London house prices are  certainly flattening and I'm sure  that'll be the case given some of the  uncertainties around brexit particularly  if people from overseas aren't buying  properties in London anymore now you  know many local Londoners might say that  that's a great thing to happen so we're  certainly looking to the future with  continued confidence just aware as  everybody should be that things can  change so your share prices off  something like nine percent this evening  because of these comments allegedly  you've been buying shares this evening  you'll notice that well I mean you know  we've got at best ever result we've got  a really strong business and we look to  the future with confidence given that  we're so confident about that outlook  and the quality of our business and  given where the share price is I'm  delighted to be able to do that wish I  could have done some more to be honest  now separately you've been at the  forefront of the campaign to get more  women into senior roles in the finance  industry which has a shocking record on  the gender pay gap what did you make of  those numbers that the BBC published  last week well I mean I think that  regardless of what we think about the  numbers which weren't good I think the  fact that they're out in the open and  public will make a difference and so you  know I think that it should be a  requirement for the gender pay gap to be  published for all organizations and we  published ours it's not as good as I  want it to be but at least it's out  there so that we can start to make a  difference TSB have done the same thing  and I applaud them for it but not very  many organizations have and I think that  until organisations are required to  disclose their gender pay gap people  will hide behind that you know that veil  if you like let's make sure that all  organisations disclose it and then it we  can change it

